course: dictionary
lesson: 1742
title: The clapperboard
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A clapperboard is a board with a hinged stick on top. At the start of each shot, someone claps the stick down onto the board in front of the camera. The board shows the scene and take number, so everyone knows which shot it is.
explain: Why the clap? It makes a sharp sound at the exact moment the stick meets the board, and the picture shows the stick closing at that same moment. Later, editors use this to line up the sound with the picture. Words like «clap» and «sound» in the lesson are clues to its job.
explain: A take is one try at filming a shot. So when someone shouts «Scene four, take two!», they mean scene four, second try. Context tells you what the words mean even if you have never seen a film set.
explain: Clapboard is a different meaning. In North America, a clapboard is one of the long, thin boards that overlap on the outside of a wooden house. The spelling is almost the same, so read the sentence around it to decide which meaning is meant.
fact: A clapperboard is a board with a hinged stick that is clapped shut at the start of each shot.
fact: The clapperboard shows the scene and take number.
fact: The clap makes a clear sound at the exact moment the sticks meet, which helps editors match the sound to the picture.
fact: A take is one try at filming a shot.
fact: In North America, clapboard means long, thin, overlapping boards on the outside of wooden houses.
exercise: «A clapperboard shows the scene and take number.» What does the clapperboard show? | The price of the camera | The weather on the day | *The scene and take number | The actors' names only
exercise: Why does the clap help editors? | It is a signal for lunch | It tells the actors to go home | *It makes a clear sound at the exact moment the sticks meet | It stops the camera from filming
exercise: Someone shouts «Scene four, take two!» What does take two mean? | The second scene of the film | *The second try at filming that shot | Two actors are on screen together | The camera has broken
test: In a wooden house in the United States, the outside walls may be covered with clapboard. What is clapboard here? | A loud clap at a party | *Long, thin, overlapping boards on a wooden house | A kind of petrol station | A stick clapped in front of a camera
test: Why do editors need the clap from the clapperboard? | To close the camera lens | To check the price of the film | *To match up the picture and the sound | To make the actors laugh
exam: «A take is one try at filming a shot.» Which word means one try at filming a shot? | scene | editor | clapboard | *take
